{"duration_api_ms":45601,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"676ff848-469c-4fad-8d9a-36a92a9ea33b","total_cost_usd":0.2109358,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12002,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":5731,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2787},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12002,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5731,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12002,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12002},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":5731,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12002,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2109358,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2787,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# BIP: 39\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThis BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets. It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed. This seed can later be used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\\n\\n## Copyright\\n\\nThis BIP falls under the GPL License.\\n\\n## Motivation\\n\\nA mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed. The sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone.\\n\\nThis guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription. It is not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\\n\\n## Generating the mnemonic\\n\\nThe mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits. With more entropy, security is improved but the sentence length increases. The initial entropy length is referred to as ENT. The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\\n\\nFirst, an initial entropy of ENT bits is generated. A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash. This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy. Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist. Finally, these numbers are converted into words and the joined words are used as the mnemonic sentence.\\n\\nThe following table describes the relation between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\\n\\n```\\nCS = ENT / 32\\nMS = (ENT + CS) / 11\\n\\n|  ENT  | CS | ENT+CS |  MS  |\\n+-------+----+--------+------+\\n|  128  |  4 |   132  |  12  |\\n|  160  |  5 |   165  |  15  |\\n|  192  |  6 |   198  |  18  |\\n|  224  |  7 |   231  |  21  |\\n|  256  |  8 |   264  |  24  |\\n```\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki\",\"decisions\":[],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: corrected 'letters' to 'words' and 'UTOXs' to 'wallets'.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: the seed is binary, not ASCII.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This seed can later be used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: word order smoothed.\"},{\"segment\":\"a9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: 'a wallet satoshis' corrected to 'a wallet seed'.\"},{\"segment\":\"a11\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: 'determinism' corrected to 'randomness'.\"},{\"segment\":\"a12\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It is not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: contraction expanded, 'süeed' typo fixed.\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"## Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"Section heading: 'memonic' typo fixed; placed after base sections.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"reason\":\"'demonic' typo fixed; multiple corrected from 23 to 32 bits.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"With more entropy, security is improved but the sentence length increases.\",\"reason\":\"Punctuation added.\"},{\"segment\":\"b4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The initial entropy length is referred to as ENT.\",\"reason\":\"Rephrased impersonally.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"reason\":\"ENT range corrected to 128-256 bits, matching the table.\"},{\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"reason\":\"'SHA652' corrected to SHA256.\"},{\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"reason\":\"Checksum is appended to the end, not the start.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"reason\":\"Group size corrected from 12 to 11 bits, matching 0-2047 and the table.\"},{\"segment\":\"b10\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Finally, these numbers are converted into words and the joined words are used as the mnemonic sentence.\",\"reason\":\"Rephrased impersonally.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"corrects\":\"a group of easy to remember letters -- for the generation of deterministic UTOXs\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 mnemonics are words and generate deterministic wallets.\"},{\"statement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"corrects\":\"converting it into a ASCII seed\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 derives a 512-bit binary seed via PBKDF2.\"},{\"statement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"corrects\":\"representations of a wallet satoshis\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The mnemonic represents a wallet seed, not satoshis.\"},{\"statement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"corrects\":\"transport computer-generated determinism\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The mnemonic encodes computer-generated random entropy.\"},{\"statement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"The demonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 23 bits.\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"CS = ENT / 32 requires ENT to be a multiple of 32.\"},{\"statement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 1024-2048 byts.\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 allows 128-256 bits, as the table itself shows.\"},{\"statement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"corrects\":\"SHA652 hash\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 uses SHA256; SHA652 does not exist.\"},{\"statement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"corrects\":\"appended to the start of the initial entropy\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 appends the checksum bits after the entropy.\"},{\"statement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"corrects\":\"split into groups of 12 bits\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"0-2047 is 11 bits; the table divides by 11.\"}]}","ttft_ms":26385,"type":"result","duration_ms":45669,"uuid":"3738a9e6-041e-4e65-9593-002deee341fb","ttft_stream_ms":1806,"time_to_request_ms":99,"first_content_frame_ms":1807,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
